# DESCRIPTION

rge drives a Realtek RTL8126 5 Gb/s Ethernet controller, as the Orange Pi 6 Plus has two of, and serves it as an Ethernet device, ether(3).
devmgr(8) starts one for each device that matches its record (driver(6)):

```text
driver rge
class net
match pci 10ec:8126
```

It knows the chip's two revisions, RTL8126_1 and RTL8126_2, by the hardware revision in its transmit configuration register, and refuses any other.
It takes the chip from its management firmware, powers the PHY, stops the MAC's microcontroller, resets the chip and sets it up as OpenBSD's and FreeBSD's rge driver does, then has the PHY advertise every speed from 10 Mb/s to 5 Gb/s, with pause, and negotiate.
On an RTL8126_2 it loads Realtek's code as FreeBSD's driver does, each part only if the chip doesn't already hold that version: the MAC microcontroller's breakpoints, while the microcontroller is stopped, and after the PHY's reset the PHY's RAM code, inside the PHY's patch handshake, then the PHY settings that go with it.
EEE, its advanced mode, ALDPS and Gigabit Lite are off, and so are checksum and VLAN offloads.
The address is the one the chip holds.
It takes the frames sent to that address, broadcasts, and every multicast.

Its receive and transmit rings each have 256 descriptors with a 2 KiB buffer each, in memory the chip reaches through its own DMA, which the IOMMU translates.
A frame of more than 1514 bytes, without its FCS, is neither sent nor taken: one that arrives spanning buffers is dropped.
The chip's interrupt is MSI-X where devmgr gave the driver an MSI vector, else INTx; with INTx the driver also polls the rings every 50 ms against a lost interrupt.
It reads the link's state when the chip says it changed, and says so on the console: `rge: <address> link up, <speed> Mb/s` or `link down`.
A frame written when every transmit descriptor is with the chip is not sent, and counts as an output error.

# FILES

The driver's tree is served in devmgr(8)'s, at `drivers/rge-<address>`.

: `ether`
  The device's ether(3) directory, which devmgr binds at `/net/etherN`.
: `status`
  Read only.
  `chip` (its revision), `mac`, `link` (`up` and the speed in Mb/s, or `down`), `code` (what starting the chip did with Realtek's code, `MAC` and `PHY` each `loaded`, `held` when the chip had that version already, as after a warm restart from another system that loaded it, or `none`) and `interrupt` (`MSI-X` or `INTx`), a line each; then the counts: `received <n> transmitted <n> dropped <n> errors <n> unsent <n>` and `interrupts <n> polls <n> system-errors <n>`.
  `dropped` counts frames received in error, spanning buffers or too short; `errors`, frames the chip failed to send; `unsent`, frames with no transmit descriptor free.
  If setup failed, `FAILED: status <n>`, with a line before it saying why when the chip's revision is unknown.

`ether`'s `ifstats` reads as the counts' first line.

